    <body>
      <section eId="sec_1-1">
        <num>1-1</num>
        <heading>Governmental powers generally</heading>
        <paragraph eId="sec_1-1__para_1" class="indent-1">
          <content>
            <p>The Government of Puerto Rico has the following rights over persons within its limits, to be exercised in the cases and in the manner provided by law:</p>
          </content>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph eId="sec_1-1__para_2" class="indent-2">
          <num>(1)</num>
          <content>
            <p>(1) To punish for crime.</p>
          </content>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph eId="sec_1-1__para_3" class="indent-2">
          <num>(2)</num>
          <content>
            <p>(2) To imprison or confine for the protection of the public peace or health, or of individual life or safety.</p>
          </content>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph eId="sec_1-1__para_4" class="indent-2">
          <num>(3)</num>
          <content>
            <p>(3) To establish custody and restraint of persons suffering from mental alienation, of drunkards, and other persons of unsound mind.</p>
          </content>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph eId="sec_1-1__para_5" class="indent-2">
          <num>(4)</num>
          <content>
            <p>(4) To establish custody and restraint of paupers for the purpose of their maintenance.</p>
          </content>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph eId="sec_1-1__para_6" class="indent-2">
          <num>(5)</num>
          <content>
            <p>(5) To establish custody and restraint of minors unprovided for by parents or natural guardians, for the purposes of their education, reformation and maintenance.</p>
          </content>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph eId="sec_1-1__para_7" class="indent-2">
          <num>(6)</num>
          <content>
            <p>(6) To require services of persons, with or without compensation, in militia duty; in jury duty; as witnesses; as city, town or village officers; in maintaining the public peace; in enforcing the service of process; in protecting life and property from fire, pestilence, wreck and flood; and in such other cases as are provided by law.</p>
          </content>
        </paragraph>
      </section>
